<title>Infants with Vomiting: Initial Diagnostic Strategy.</title>
<graphic href="eee0844-w0001.gif" />
<cell type="start" id="cell-1">
<label>Vomiting infant &gt;3 months old</label>
<path target="cell-2">Bilious vomiting</path>
<path target="cell-3">Intermittent vomiting</path>
<path target="cell-4">Nonbilious, projectile vomiting</path>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-2">
<label>Consider sepsis, obstruction (midgut volvulus, left-sided microcolon, meconium plug syndrome), or colostrum regurgitation.</label>
<path target="cell-5"></path>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-5">
<label>Order abdominal X ray; consider upper GI with contrast and fluoroscopy.</label>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-3">
<label>Consider reflux, gastric ulcer, or volvulus. Order upper GI.</label>
</cell>
<cell type="decision" id="cell-4">
<label>Nasogastric tube decompression; palpable olive?</label>
<path target="cell-6">Yes</path>
<path target="cell-7">No</path>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-6">
<label>Give IV fluids and perform pyloromyotomy.</label>
</cell>
<cell type="decision" id="cell-7">
<label>Abdominal ultrasound.</label>
<path target="cell-6">Pyloric wall &gt;3-4 mm thick or &gt;18 mm long</path>
<path target="cell-8">Not consistent with pyloric stenosis</path>
</cell>
<cell type="action" id="cell-8">
<label>Reassess and consider upper GI.</label>
</cell>
</algorithm>
